well obviously it's been frustrating to  get pretty close but not actually to win  it but I'm very proud of the campaign  that I fought and we've built a real  momentum during the campaign but look  we've now reached an outcome and it's  very important for us to unite as a  party and to start the fight back as Tim  said in his speech and I'm confident we  could do that does this mean the party  is now heading to the left and towards  some kind of working relationship with  labour well I think the outcome of the  election with quite a close result  demonstrates that there are many people  in the party you who have sort of strong  views about the issues that I raised  during the campaign and it's very  important that they're reflected as I'm  sure they will be as we go forward so I  want us to be a liberal party a radical  Liberal Party and that doesn't sit very  comfortably in the left-right spectrum  we will challenge both labour and the  Conservatives and fundamentally it's  about giving power to people and power  to communities and that's what we will  be about the reason why I ask is because  David Steele the former leader who was  praised by Tim Farron tonight has  already mooted that idea of establishing  some kind of partnership with the Labour  Party well my view is a liberal and as a  progressive is that progressives across  politics should be prepared to talk to  each other we should not be tribal in  our politics absolutely indeed last week  I met with Caroline Lucas to talk about  how we could work together in  Westminster on the areas where we agree  on the absolute imperative of tackling  climate change on civil liberties on on  Europe but we should be prepared to talk  to people in the labour party as well  but they're going through quite a  tortured election campaign themselves  with what looks like quite a lot of  acrimony and I think it's worth us just  standing back from that for a while  and building our own brand and our our  own values and demonstrating those to  the British people that's I think what  our greatest priority was being Tim  Farron is also a committed evangelical  Christian he's had what some would see  as a small C conservative voting record  on issues such as gay marriage and also  abortion is that compatible with the  sort of liberalism you've been telling  me about well I think tim has been very  clear that he won't allow his faith to  in any way interfere with his leadership  of the party we must always respect  people of faith I happen to be very  different  I'm not fortunate enough in a way to  have a profound faith but the important  thing is liberal values and making sure  that we speak for all the millions of  people across our country who actually  share those liberal values and need a  voice in Parliament what do you see your  role now as the runner-up in this  contest as being well the first thing is  to get a holiday I haven't had a day off  since the 1st of January so it's a bit  Vaccari to be honest and I think just  getting a bit of rest recuperation  spending some time with Mary my wife  watching my team Norwich City will give  me a better perspective on things but  look I'm determined to play my part in  the fight back and I have things that  I'm passionate about I will continue my  fight for equality for those people who  suffer from mental ill-health  something that I care very deeply about  these things aren't going to stop simply  because I haven't won this election you  were a minister and I think many people  regarded you as a strong minister and  minister I knew what he was doing when  it came to the briefs in the health  department nonetheless do you think it  was a mistake for the Liberal Democrats  to enter into that coalition with the  Conservatives no absolutely not I think  we acted fundamentally in the national  interest I think the country is in a  much better place now than it was five  years ago and I think what we will now  see is what a conservative  government is like unconstrained by the  Liberal Democrats so for example in the  budget we've already seen quite a  dramatic cut in the income of people in  work on low incomes as a result of the  cut of the tax credit so this is a very  different government to the coalition  and there's so many things that we  should be proud of the pupil premium  which gets two and a half billion pounds  into schools for children for the  poorest background my work on mental  health cutting tax for people on low pay  these are liberal values that we  implemented in government and we should  be proud of them  Norman lamb thank you
 

